WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:08.340
In the early hours of a September morning,
1940, this plane, A Heinkel He 111, was making

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:14.719
it’s way towards London, carrying 2 tonnes
of bombs to the densely populated city.

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:21.180
It may well have reached its destination,
if not for the early warning systems the British

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.910
Military Home Defence had created.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:30.510
A network of radar stations, all feeding into
a central operations room where incoming raid

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:36.280
information was ratified and mapped on a gigantic
chart of Britain.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:43.340
It was from this room in Bentley Prior that
all RAF commands, during the Battle of Britain,

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.489
were made.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.030
This was a frantic, but precise procedure.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:53.140
Every minute that passed was a crucial minute
stolen from the pilots.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.719
Vital time needed to gain altitude to meet
the enemy on a level playing field.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:04.890
This groundbreaking air defence network allowed
Britain's defenders to be in the air, at interception

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:11.520
altitude, within 10 minutes of the first sign
of invasion on the radar screens.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.210
This would not have been possible without
aircraft capable of quickly gaining altitude

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.060
to meet their foes.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:25.540
The Spitfire is the icon of the Battle of
Britain, capable of climbing 2500 feet (760

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:32.840
metres) per minute, taking them, at minimum,
4 to 5 minutes to reach their target’s altitude.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.090
The Spitfire was a nimple interceptor.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:41.510
A plane that could rapidly gain altitude,
and proceed to fiercely defend its airspace.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:47.670
To do this it needed to be powerful, manoeuvrable,
and capable of packing a punch.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.439
Some of the greatest engineers of World War
2 worked on this fighter and the designs they

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:57.509
came up with changed the course of the war.

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:08.069
This is the insane engineering of the Spitfire.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:13.720
Despite its patriotic fame, the groundbreaking
nature of the Spitfire’s design is difficult

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.840
to comprehend today.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.970
In a world where we are long accustomed to
all-metal low wing fighters, the Spitfire,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.870
despite its undeniable elegance, may seem
mundane.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:32.440
However, when the spitfire first swept through
airfields in 1936, the RAF still operated

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.110
aircraft like the Gloster Gauntlet and the
Hawker Fury

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.110
Biplanes were the norm for fighters of this
era.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:45.099
The stacked wings increased the lift the aircraft
could generate while making them stronger

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:49.200
and better able to withstand the g-forces
of manoeuvres.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.620
But they generated a lot of drag.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:59.280
The Spitfire was made possible by new powerful
engines and new revolutionary aged hardened

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.810
aluminium alloys, freeing the Spitfire’s
chief designer RJ Mitchell to get creative,

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:12.489
setting out to create a plane capable of out-running
and out turning any foe.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:17.820
Turn performance is essentially measured by
the minimum turn radius a plane can achieve.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:23.160
A tighter turn radius gives a fighter aircraft
an edge in a dog fight.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:29.270
To understand what effects the radius of this
turn we first need to understand how an aircraft

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.790
turns.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:36.870
To begin a turn an aircraft will roll in the
direction of the turn, this is called the

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.830
aircraft bank angle.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:44.900
This splits the lift the plane is generating
into two components: a horizontal component

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.980
that causes the plane to turn and the vertical
component that keeps the plane in the sky.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:57.409
A steeper bank angle will increase the horizontal
component and decrease the turn radius, while

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:00.349
stealing lift from the vertical components.

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:05.870
This vertical component needs to equal the
weight of the aircraft, or the plane will

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.260
lose altitude.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.900
To compensate for that the pilot will deflect
the plane's elevators downwards to increase

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.690
lift, but this increases drag.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:22.229
This increase in drag means the pilot will
also need to increase engine power to maintain

00:04:22.229 --> 00:04:23.279
speed.

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:29.439
The maximum turn possible that won’t result
in loss of altitude or speed is called the

00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:35.240
sustained turn performance, and it’s affected
by the weight of the aircraft, the excess

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.909
power available from the engine, and the design
of the wing.

00:04:39.909 --> 00:04:46.169
To excel in battle the Spitfire needed the
perfect wing, and Mitchell did his absolute

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:48.690
best to give it just that.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:55.050
RJ Mitchell had plenty of experience in designing
sleek high performance aircraft, designing

00:04:55.050 --> 00:05:00.990
the Supermarine S.5 that won Schneider Trophy
race in 1928.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:06.599
There was some deliberation on the shape the
Spitfire’s wing would take, with some early

00:05:06.599 --> 00:05:12.919
designs, like this one from 1934, showing
a straight taper, unlike the iconic elliptical

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:15.379
wing it eventually took.

00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:21.360
This would have been vastly easier to manufacture,
but the shape a wing takes has a huge effect

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.550
on performance.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:30.039
In particular, lift distribution, and the
elliptical wing provides the ideal lift distribution

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:32.740
to minimise induced drag.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:37.649
Induced drag occurs when high pressure air
from underneath the wing travels to mix with

00:05:37.649 --> 00:05:41.860
low pressure air above the wing, over the
wing tip.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:48.199
This creates a vortice at the wing tip that
saps kinetic energy away from the plane.

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:52.379
This elliptical lift distribution minimises
this effect,

00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:58.409
but RJ Mitchell is quoted in Alfred Price’s
“The Spitfire Story” saying this:

00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:06.250
“I don't give a bugger whether it's elliptical
or not, so long as it covers the guns”

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:13.169
The reduction in induced drag is often cited
as the sole benefit of the elliptical wing,

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:19.189
when in actuality it barely made a dent in
the overall drag characteristics of the plane,

00:06:19.189 --> 00:06:22.749
which was not worth the added difficulty in
manufacturing.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:23.749
[2]

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:29.589
The real benefits of the elliptical wing came
with the planform's slow reduction in chord

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:30.589
length.

00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:32.520
Take a straight tapered wing.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:38.490
Its chord, the width of the wing, steadily
decreases from the root of the wing.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:44.449
Steadily reducing the area near the fuselage
that can be used to fit equipment.

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:50.219
The curve of the ellipse on the other hand
maintains the width of the wing close to the

00:06:50.219 --> 00:06:57.479
fuselage and then drops off more rapidly towards
the wing tips, this provides ample room inside

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:04.409
the wing to fit the landing gear, guns, hydraulics,
radiators and wing support structures.

00:07:04.409 --> 00:07:11.219
The room provided to fit a strong, but lightweight
wing spar is a key benefit that allowed the

00:07:11.219 --> 00:07:15.789
Spitfire to take daring turns without worry
of structural failure.

00:07:15.789 --> 00:07:21.689
The engineers of the Spitfire had a delicate
balancing act to perform.

00:07:21.689 --> 00:07:27.709
Increasing the strength of a wing often requires
adding weight, and adding weight decreases

00:07:27.709 --> 00:07:32.419
turn performance, climb performance and increases
fuel consumption.

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:39.279
The Spitfire’s wing is not perfectly elliptical,
its trailing edge turns inwards more rapidly

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:41.479
than the leading edge.

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:44.930
This was done primarily for structural reasons.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:51.529
The main structural support of the wing is
the wing spar and in the Spitfire it runs

00:07:51.529 --> 00:07:55.610
perfectly along the quarter chord of the Spitfires
wing.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:08:01.499
The quarter chord is located, as its name
would suggest, a quarter of the way up the

00:08:01.499 --> 00:08:02.899
width of the wing.

00:08:02.899 --> 00:08:10.259
It is the aerodynamic centre of the wing,
where the overall lift of the wing acts through.

00:08:10.259 --> 00:08:16.479
If the wing was perfectly elliptical it would
be impossible to place this wing spar along

00:08:16.479 --> 00:08:24.449
the quarter chord and the overall lift force
would, as a result, be behind the wing spar,

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:27.029
instead of directly over it.

00:08:27.029 --> 00:08:32.759
This would create a twisting motion on the
wing that would require additional strengthening

00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:34.190
and weight.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:40.019
This is why the trailing edge curves in as
it does, to adjust the quarter chord’s location

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:42.810
to be in line with the wing spar.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.790
Much was done to minimise the weight of these
support structures.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:52.360
The bending force the wing needs to resist
decreases as we travel down the wing, so the

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:57.380
spar can reduce in strength and weight through
the wingspan.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:02.339
Manufacturing this kind of shape was not particularly
easy in the 1940s.

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:08.170
Today we can manufacture single parts to shapes
engineers of world war two could only imagine

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:10.720
using CNC milling machines.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.889
To allow the strength of the wing spar to
decrease, it’s constructed from simple nested

00:09:15.889 --> 00:09:17.629
square extrusions.

00:09:17.629 --> 00:09:24.199
The upper and lower spar booms were constructed
from 5 concentric aluminium square sections,

00:09:24.199 --> 00:09:27.990
which were cut and placed inside of each other
like this.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:34.399
This wing was an engineering masterpiece that
gave the Spitfire incredible handling characteristics.

00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:40.430
When we compare the Spitfire to the BF109,
its nemesis during the Battle of Britain,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:45.950
we can start to make some real deductions
about the effect this wing had on the plane.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:50.060
The wing area of the Spit is noticeably larger.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:53.580
This affects a performance metric called wing
loading.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.459
Which is simply the total mass of the aircraft
divided by the wing area.

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:01.050
This affects the turning radius of the aircraft.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:07.120
A heavy aircraft with small wings will have
a large wing loading, and as a result will

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.870
have a large turning radius.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:16.490
The BF109’s wings were much smaller and
thus it had a higher wing load, and so the

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:20.529
BF109 had a larger turning radius than the
Spitfire. [2]

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:26.410
While the space afforded by the Spitfire’s
wings allowed it fit 8 Browning machine guns

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:27.910
inside the wing,

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:37.189
or as later variants featured, 2 larger 20
mm cannon and four of the smaller 7.7 mm Brownings

00:10:37.189 --> 00:10:39.040
all mounted inside the wing,

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.470
and outside the propellor arc, which reduced
the engineer effort needed to integrate them.

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:47.810
A luxury the BF109 did not have.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:53.550
The engineers at Messerschmitt were forced
to use more complicated designs to integrate

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:54.550
armament.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:59.000
Two machine guns mounted above the engine
could fire through the propeller blades with

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:04.379
an interrupter gear timing the triggering
to ensure they did not strike the blades.

00:11:04.379 --> 00:11:09.209
A cannon could be fitted behind the engine,
which would fire through a hollow tube that

00:11:09.209 --> 00:11:14.819
ran through the inverted V-12 engine and through
the nose of the propeller hub.

00:11:14.819 --> 00:11:19.990
Later variants attempted to correct the disparity
in fire power by mounting guns inside the

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:25.910
wings, but there wasn’t enough room for
ammunition, so a belt feeder was run through

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:29.480
a shoot from the fuselage to the guns.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.839
This wasn’t a reliable mechanism.

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:37.389
Some variants included gun pods mounted below
the wing, which negatively affected the plane's

00:11:37.389 --> 00:11:38.389
handling.

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:41.399
The Spitfire’s primary role was an interceptor.

00:11:41.399 --> 00:11:45.639
To do this it needed a powerful engine and
light airframe.

00:11:45.639 --> 00:11:50.089
The most important factor for climb rate is
power to weight ratio.

00:11:50.089 --> 00:11:54.320
Creating a powerful engine that could fit
into a small airframe required some of the

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.220
best minds in Britain.

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:01.600
The Merlin engine of the Spitfire went through
many iterations over the war, with the constant

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:08.209
goal to squeeze more and more power out of
the 27 litre displacement engine.

00:12:08.209 --> 00:12:12.139
Displacement meaning the volume of the cylinder
swept by each piston.

00:12:12.139 --> 00:12:16.899
The displacement of an engine has a huge effect
on the total power output.

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:21.620
Power is derived from fuel burning to create
heat, which creates pressure on the cylinders

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:22.899
to do work.

00:12:22.899 --> 00:12:28.249
To increase power we need to increase the
amount of heat energy released, but we can’t

00:12:28.249 --> 00:12:32.210
just add more fuel without adding more air
to burn it.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:36.019
There is an optimum air fuel ratio, about
12-1.

00:12:36.019 --> 00:12:39.199
12 parts air to 1 part fuel.

00:12:39.199 --> 00:12:45.620
This gets even more complicated for aircraft
that fly at various altitudes, because the

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.339
air pressure decreases as we climb.

00:12:48.339 --> 00:12:51.819
Air pressure drops by half at 5,500 metres.

00:12:51.819 --> 00:12:56.160
Which would half the oxygen available to burn
the fuel .

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:01.670
To combat this the Merlin featured a supercharger
to increase air pressure.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:06.430
Air began its journey into the Merlin engine
through the air intake located underneath

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:07.800
the fuselage.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.790
From here it first passes the carburetor where
fuel is mixed into the airstream.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:15.980
The Spitfire utilised a float carburetor.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:19.800
Float carburetors use, as the name would suggest,
a float.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:24.980
The float works similarly to the float in
your toilet cistern, detecting the fluid level

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:30.740
and controlling a valve to keep it at the
optimum level in the carburetor tank.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:35.790
This became an issue for the Spitfire when
German pilots learned the engine would cut

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:38.499
out in a negative g manoeuvre.

00:13:38.499 --> 00:13:44.319
A negative g manoeuvre would force the float
down and open the valve, flooding the engine

00:13:44.319 --> 00:13:46.629
with too much fuel.

00:13:46.629 --> 00:13:53.319
The BF109 engine utilised direct fuel injection,
a more complicated system that required 12

00:13:53.319 --> 00:13:59.389
plungers, 1 for each cylinder, cams to time
the fuel injection, and geared power from

00:13:59.389 --> 00:14:00.389
the engine.

00:14:00.389 --> 00:14:06.589
A more complicated solution that allowed German
pilots to perform negative g dives to evade

00:14:06.589 --> 00:14:12.870
pursuit, while Spitfire pilots needed extra
time to perform a roll before diving, which

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:19.079
ensured the dive was made in positive g, giving
German pilots precious time to escape.

00:14:19.079 --> 00:14:20.079
[3]

00:14:20.079 --> 00:14:24.980
After passing this carburettor the air entered
the supercharger compressor, where the air

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:28.360
was compressed before entering the piston.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.839
Increasing the power output of the engine.

00:14:30.839 --> 00:14:36.680
This system helped the Spitfire squeeze out
extra horsepower from their 27 litre engine,

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:44.829
rivalling the power of the 34 litre DB 601
engine fitted into BF109s during the Battle

00:14:44.829 --> 00:14:45.829
of Britain.

00:14:45.829 --> 00:14:51.570
However, early Merlin engines featured a single
speed, single stage supercharger.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:57.389
The speed of the supercharger being determined
by the gearing ratio coming from the crankshaft.

00:14:57.389 --> 00:15:03.199
This forced the engineers to design the supercharger
to work at the optimum speed and compression

00:15:03.199 --> 00:15:06.269
ratio for a particular altitude.

00:15:06.269 --> 00:15:12.069
We can see this when looking at power curves
that plot engine power vs altitude.

00:15:12.069 --> 00:15:18.660
The early Merlin III reached peak power around
10,000 feet, before quickly dropping off.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:24.050
You may notice that power output is actually
lower at sea-level, despite the ambient air

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:25.980
pressure being higher here.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:31.649
That’s also a result of the supercharger
speed being fixed, in order to prevent the

00:15:31.649 --> 00:15:36.850
engine from being over pressured there was
a throttle on the air intake that gradually

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:40.089
opened as the plane gained altitude.

00:15:40.089 --> 00:15:46.399
While the Spitfire had excellent power at
its optimum altitude, its BF109 foes during

00:15:46.399 --> 00:15:51.129
the Battle of Britain had superior power at
lower altitude.

00:15:51.129 --> 00:15:57.420
Because they featured an ingenious device
that allowed the supercharger to vary its

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:58.420
speed.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:04.231
Their supercharger was placed at a 90 degree
angle and power was transferred to it with

00:16:04.231 --> 00:16:05.809
a fluid coupling.

00:16:05.809 --> 00:16:10.880
Which uses a fluid to transfer power from
the engine to the supercharger.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:15.879
The input shaft causes a fluid inside the
coupling to rotate around the casing with

00:16:15.879 --> 00:16:22.339
a pump, which in turn causes a turbine attached
to the output shaft to turn.

00:16:22.339 --> 00:16:28.279
This method of power transmission allowed
the supercharger to operate at various speeds,

00:16:28.279 --> 00:16:33.790
because the amount of oil inside the coupling
was controlled by a separate pump which gradually

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:38.749
increased the amount of oil inside the coupling
as the plane gained altitude.

00:16:38.749 --> 00:16:46.089
[4] This prevented the supercharger from overpressuring
the engine and gave the BF109 engine superior

00:16:46.089 --> 00:16:48.879
power at lower altitudes.

00:16:48.879 --> 00:16:54.949
This gave the BF 109 E variant, which was
most widely used during the Battle of Britain,

00:16:54.949 --> 00:16:57.009
an edge over the Spitfire.

00:16:57.009 --> 00:17:03.040
While the Spitfire could out turn the 109,
the 109 had a better power to weight ratio,

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:09.480
which allowed the 109 to sustain a climbing
turn to out manoeuvre a Spitfire on its tail.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:15.439
British engineers were well aware of this
issue prior to the Battle of Britain and development

00:17:15.439 --> 00:17:22.360
began on a two stage, two speed supercharger
for the Merlin engine in mid 1940.

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:28.559
This allowed the supercharger to operate at
two different speeds via a gear change, but

00:17:28.559 --> 00:17:34.809
also introduced a second impeller which increased
the compression for even better high altitude

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:35.820
performance.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:41.190
This increased compression caused an increase
in temperature of the air that would negatively

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:47.090
affect engine efficiency, and so an intercooler
also had to be introduced to cool the supercharged

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.440
air before entering the piston.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.590
This intercooler removed 33% of the heat added
by the supercharger.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:18:00.110
Removing more heat would have been beneficial
but there is a balancing act here.

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:05.760
To remove more heat, the plane needed a larger
radiator to dump that heat to atmosphere and

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:11.260
a larger radiator would result in more drag,
which negated the power increase.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:16.950
The radiator design was in fact one of the
chief ways the Spitfire managed to squeeze

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:20.770
so much power out of its smaller Merlin engine.

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:26.590
German engineers had noted how much smaller
the Spitfires radiators were, and knew exactly

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:27.590
why.

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:32.950
In one meeting between the chiefs of Germany’s
aviation development Messerschmitt noted that

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:38.460
the Spitfires radiators were half the size
of their own, while the head of German engine

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:44.470
development explained to an indignant government
official, that to reduce the radiator size

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:50.500
would require higher coolant pressure which
their engine couldn’t tolerate without leaking.

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:55.679
The British had developed high pressure high
temperature coolant systems for their high

00:18:55.679 --> 00:19:00.520
speed racing engines to compete in races like
the Schneider Trophy.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:07.000
The Germans were prohibited from participating
in these races by the treaty of Versaille,

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:12.429
and so were left behind in the development
of this vital technology.

00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:17.510
Increasing the pressure of the coolant allowed
the engineers to raise the operating temperature

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.330
of the coolant without it boiling.

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:25.950
An increased temperature difference between
the radiator and the outside air increased

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:32.399
the rate heat could radiate away from the
plane, and this allowed the Spitfires radiator

00:19:32.399 --> 00:19:34.450
to be much smaller.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:40.090
This did require the radiators to be stronger
and thus heavier, but the reduction in drag

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.570
more than made up for the increased weight.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:49.409
The BF109 and Spitfire were formidable foes
to each other, and the difference between

00:19:49.409 --> 00:19:56.100
life and death more often than not came down
to the skill of the pilots and the strategic

00:19:56.100 --> 00:20:00.670
advantages the British had in fighting over
their home soil.

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:06.740
The Battle of Britain was won on small margins
and the engineers of the Spitfire did everything

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:10.370
they could to give the Spitfire an edge in
battle.

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:16.350
But it and the much more numerous Hawker Hurricane
would not have stood a chance without the

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:22.200
systems and procedures on the ground that
allowed them to get into the air, day after

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:23.200
day.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:29.160
Radar, data processing, mapping, chain of
commands, pilot training and even fuel octane

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:36.320
ratings all played a role in winning the battle
of Britain, and I explain it all in our Nebula

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:39.700
Original series “The Battle of Britain”.

